Q1
1. Simplicity
When one class has 600 lines and does many different jobs, the code becomes difficult to understand. For example, changing the user interface may affect the network code.
Testability
It is difficult to test the class because it depends on the server, user interface, and local storage. Testing one feature may require setting up all these parts.
Low-cost maintenance
If the server response format changes, we may need to modify the large class in several places. Separating responsibilities makes changes easier.
What would I move first?
I would move the network request and its related logic into a separate repository. This would keep network operations separate from the user interface and make the code easier to test.

Q2
First, understand the existing application and identify what each class does.
Identify the main problems and responsibilities that are mixed together.
Learn the available architectural patterns and understand their differences.
Choose a pattern that fits the needs of the project.
Separate responsibilities gradually and test the changes.
The first step must be understanding the existing application before choosing a pattern.
“Use MVVM everywhere” is not a good answer because every project has different requirements. A small application may not need a complex architecture, while a larger application may need a different structure.
